1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,131
Mid-winter in Antarctica. The sun went down weeks ago and has not come back yet.

2
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,735
Look up.

3
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,984
Ribbons of green southern lights dance below the stars. Now look down. You are not alone.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,392
You are standing right inside a giant crowd of father emperor penguins, shoulder to feathered shoulder.

5
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,860
Each father balances one precious egg on top of his feet, tucked under a warm fold of belly feathers.

6
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:01,813
Feel the crowd shuffle past you in tiny steps. Every penguin takes a turn on the freezing outside, then moves into the cozy middle.

7
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,214
In the center of the huddle, it can feel as warm as a mild spring afternoon.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,551
Listen to your left, and then behind you. Trumpeting calls echo across the dark sea ice.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,483
Every penguin's voice is different, like yours.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,346
With no nests or street signs on flat ice, that special voice is how families find each other again.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,233
Now look straight ahead toward the far flat line of the ice. A pale pink ribbon is glowing in the dark.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,613
They have not eaten for months. They're waiting for this.

13
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,441
Returning light means the mothers are marching back from the ocean with bellies full of fish for the new chicks.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,729
Together, patience and shared warmth carried life through the longest night on Earth.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,263
Watch real wild penguins live right now on our Antipodes Island and Ōamaru penguin cams at Digital Live Zoo.
